+\subsection{Directional agreement beyond the additive-noise model}
+\label{sec:num-directional}
+
+Does the curvature--noise structure also describe the direction of the real-data
+algorithm difference? In the post-hoc E5 comparison, we evaluate the Hessian
+$A_{\rm ref}$, third derivative $T_{\rm ref}$, and single-example gradient-noise
+covariance $\Sigma_{\rm ref}$ at the reference optimizer. Substituting
+$\Sigma_{\rm ref}/m$ into the control coefficient gives the plug-in vector
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:e5-proxy}
+p_{\gamma,H,m}
+=-\frac{\gamma^2}{m}\,\kappa(H)\,A_{\rm ref}^{-1}T_{\rm ref}[\Sigma_{\rm ref}],
+\qquad \kappa(H)=\frac{(H-1)(2H-1)}{12H}.
+\end{equation}
+No observed displacement is used to fit its direction or amplitude.
+
+Across all six nonzero-proxy configurations at the designated $K=64$ window,
+this vector aligns closely with the full 124-dimensional paired mean difference:
+cosine point estimates range from $0.9965$ to $0.9993$. This comparison uses all
+coordinates, not only the projection in Equation~\eqref{eq:real-projection}.
+Observed-to-proxy norm ratios range from $0.718$ to $0.940$: the proxy captures
+the direction more accurately than the magnitude and overpredicts all six
+magnitudes.
+
+This agreement supports a directional explanation based on curvature--noise
+interaction. It does not uniquely identify the control prefactor, since both
+algorithms' second-order coefficients share the same direction. At the base step
+size with $N=32,m=8$, the observed $H=4/H=2$ projected ratio is $2.927$, rather
+than $3.5$. Thus the comparison is informative beyond the idealized model
+without establishing its exact coefficient under state-dependent noise or
+certifying stationarity. Appendix~\ref{app:e5-bridge} retains all configurations,
+windows, reference-direction comparisons, and uncertainty diagnostics.
